From 6f6d0d71ba93a5078673dfd3bb114b7c66e36b26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andre Watson <dre@ligandal.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 00:08:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 42/42] perf(sm120): online FP8 for the always-on BF16 weights,
 8-bit weight-only dense path

Decode on the RTX PRO 6000 is dominated by the always-on BF16 weights
(attention and DeltaNet projections, shared expert, HyperConnection mix,
lm_head), which are read every token. This serves them as 8-bit at load time.
Slightly lossy: outputs are not token-identical to the BF16 baseline.

- SGLANG_SM120_ONLINE_MXFP8=true (default off): port of mratsim's
  sglang-qwen38fn-sm120-turbo patches 0003 and 0008. On SM120, unquantized
  linears are quantized at load (MXFP8 dense/attention projections), and the
  HyperConnection mix pair and lm_head become rowwise FP8 (one fp32 scale per
  output row). The mix pair is created on meta and ingested as FP8 so the BF16
  copy never lands in VRAM. New file kernels/ops/gemm/sm120_online_fp8.py.
- SGLANG_SM120_ONLINE_FP8_DENSE=rowwise (default mxfp8): new 8-bit weight-only
  (W8A16) path for the dense linears using a rowwise FP8 GEMV at decode sizes.
  This is the configuration that measured fastest.
- HyperConnection mix fixes: the fused mix kernel learns FP8 weights (FP8W),
  and batches of 17-128 rows now run it in 16-row slices instead of falling
  back to dequantizing the pair to BF16 every step. Prefill rows re-materialize
  BF16 operands block by block from the resident FP8 pair.
- Draft-head scale fix: when the NEXTN draft slices the shared lm_head down to
  the hot-token map, the rowwise scale is sliced with it, so the draft reads the
  same FP8 pair as the target.

Measured on a Modal RTX PRO 6000 Server Edition (600 W), cap 8, NEXTN 3/1/4:
aggregate 276.8 / 400.4 / 610.8 / 784.1 tok/s at 1/2/4/8 streams. Cost: the
500K-token time to first token rises from 48.8 s to 53.5 s. See BENCHMARKS.md.

Credit: mratsim (online FP8 port), Penny Royal / jpezzulli sglang-rtxpro6000
(FP8 measurements), gabrielolympie (W8A16 weight-only approach for low-M dense).
